#!/usr/bin/bash

Some directories here begin with . (period).
So add -aR to any ls you do to see all the files.

dwarfdump segfault in match_buildid(): build-ids compared
before their lengths (NULL deref / heap over-read on a
.build-id debug file)
 #345

dwarfdump crashes (SIGSEGV, release build) when it follows
the .build-id path for an executable and the debug file
it finds there has no build-id note. If the debug file
has a build-id that is shorter than the executable's, the
comparison reads past the end of the note (heap over-read,
seen with ASan).

exe has a 20-byte build-id and no DWARF. The candidate
dbg/.build-id/xx/yyyy.debug has no .note.gnu.build-id. It
has a .gnu_debuglink, so dwarf_gnu_debuglink() succeeds
on it. /usr/lib/debug is a default global path, so a file
like this under /usr/lib/debug/.build-id/ has the same
effect without --add-debuglink-path.

ASan build:

A second variant gives the debug file a 4-byte build-id equal to the first 4 bytes of the executable's:

$ mkdir -p dbgshort/.build-id/$D2
$ gcc -g -Wl,--build-id=0x$(echo $BID | cut -c1-8) -o dbgshort/.build-id/$D2/$REST.debug p.c
$ dwarfdump --add-debuglink-path=$PWD/dbgshort -i exe

The release build prints nothing wrong, but ASan reports:

==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow on address 0x5030000005c4 
